2026-06-15 ~16:15 PT: Skill-awareness architectural boundary established (PA + PM, verbal ratification). Question: should Piper's knowledge of its PM skills (draft-issue, sprint-plan, etc.) live in PIPER.md? Answer: no — two distinct things are needed, and the DDD boundary explains why.

(1) Skills are product capability, not user profile. They don't belong in the user's PM profile (alpha_users.preferences / PIPER.user.md) or in PIPER.md as currently written. PIPER.md is governed by ADR-059: every capability listed must have a working server-side implementation. Skills are currently prompt-layer procedures; adding them to PIPER.md would cause Piper to offer capabilities it can't yet execute on the plugin path — a broken experience.

(2) Two things are needed: a skills manifest (discovery layer — so Piper knows what skills exist and can suggest them) and skill invocation (execution layer — so Piper can run the procedure). These are separate concerns. On the native path (Claude Desktop/Code with .claude/skills/ loaded), both are already satisfied: SKILLS.md is the manifest, SKILL.md files are the procedures. On the plugin path, both are open design questions.

(3) Recommended future shape (not decided): a PIPER-SKILLS.md alongside PIPER.md in config/, governed by the same ADR-059 discipline ("every skill listed here must be executable via the plugin path"). Skills become listable in that file only after their server-side invocation path is built. Separately: skill invocation on the plugin path could use MCP resources (server reads and injects the SKILL.md on demand) or MCP tools (each skill exposed as a named tool) — the trade-offs belong in a future ADR under the BYOC/plugin track.

(4) Relation to existing issues: this boundary is the architectural "why" behind issues #1242 (MEET-PIPER-GITHUB), #1244 (CONSULT-ENRICH-FIX), and #1245 (PIPER-SKILL-MERGE), which together form the prerequisites for Wave P plugin-path skills. The architectural question of HOW skills become invokable on the plugin path is not yet decided; it lives in the BYOC track (PA-owned, pending Lead Dev feedback on prereqs).

2026-06-16 ~14:00 PT: Corrected plugin tool topology (PA, from live ToolSearch — actual MCP tool schemas). The ADR-072 brief (2026-06-15) described the plugin as having three tools (ask-piper, consult-piper, meet-piper). The actual MCP tool definitions are different:

(1) The plugin exposes 5 tools, not 3: `ask_piper(message)`, `get_profile()`, `save_profile(content)`, `get_company_profile()`, `save_company_profile(content)`. There are no separate `consult_piper` or `meet_piper` MCP tools. The conceptual "consult-piper" and "meet-piper" are user-flow names; the underlying MCP tools are the 5 above.

(2) `get_profile()` description explicitly says: "Call this at the start of meet-piper (to check if setup is needed) and from any skill that wants the user's calibration." This means Layer 1 of the defense-in-depth model (tool descriptions embedding skill-routing guidance) is partially implemented already — the tool description already tells Claude to consult `get_profile` when a skill needs calibration.

(3) `ask_piper` is the single conversational tool (natural-language PM request → intent-classified response). All enrichment behavior (what was conceptually "consult-piper") happens server-side within `ask_piper`. The Bug B failure (payload too large) is specific to the enrichment path within `ask_piper`, not a separate tool.

(4) Implication for ADR-072: the "plugin tool topology" decision (#3 in the ADR brief) should work from the actual 5-tool surface, not the conceptual 3-tool framing. The current topology already demonstrates a separation-of-concerns pattern (`ask_piper` = conversation, `get_profile`/`save_profile` = profile I/O, `get_company_profile`/`save_company_profile` = company I/O). ADR-072 decides whether to extend this topology (add per-skill tools? a meta-tool `run_skill(name)`?) or route at the server layer within `ask_piper`. Addendum sent to Arch.

