2026-08-09 12:4x PT — Arch — `ToolEffect` MUST BE IntEnum, AND THE ORDERING MUST BE ASSERTED (PA found it in my own ruling)

MY ERROR: my 09:4x read/write ruling specified `needs_consent = effect >= WRITE` / `needs_confirm =
  effect == DESTRUCTIVE` against PA's spec type `class ToolEffect(str, Enum)` — WITHOUT VERIFYING THE TYPE
  SUPPORTS THE OPERATION. I wrote executable-looking pseudocode into a ruling and four consumers (#1509,
  #1190, capability legibility, MCP annotations) were about to build on it.
PA VERIFIED BEFORE LETTING IT STAND; Arch REPRODUCED before ruling:
    StrEffect.WRITE       >= StrEffect.READ   -> True   (COINCIDENTALLY right: 'w' > 'r')
    StrEffect.DESTRUCTIVE >= StrEffect.WRITE  -> False  ← AND IT DOES NOT RAISE
    needs_consent (str,Enum): {READ: False, WRITE: True, DESTRUCTIVE: False}
    needs_consent (IntEnum):  {READ: False, WRITE: True, DESTRUCTIVE: True}
  → UNDER (str, Enum) THE MOST DANGEROUS TIER SILENTLY SKIPS THE CONSENT GATE. `str, Enum` inherits
    str.__ge__, so `>=` compares VALUES LEXICOGRAPHICALLY.

✅ IntEnum RATIFIED. 🔴 BUT THE TYPE FIX ALONE IS NOT ENOUGH — THE ORDERING IS A SAFETY INVARIANT AND MUST
  BE ASSERTED IN A TEST. THE FAILURE IS ASYMMETRIC IN THE DANGEROUS DIRECTION: WRITE >= READ is true BY
  COINCIDENCE, so the bug is INVISIBLE IN THE EASY CASE and appears only on the tier that matters. A test
  checking READ/WRITE passes and proves nothing — THE PAIR YOU'D NATURALLY TEST IS THE PAIR THAT WORKS.
  REQUIRED (DESTRUCTIVE pair first, it is the one that breaks):
      assert ToolEffect.DESTRUCTIVE > ToolEffect.WRITE > ToolEffect.READ
      assert (ToolEffect.DESTRUCTIVE >= ToolEffect.WRITE) is True   # the consent predicate itself
  One line converts a silent wrong answer into a build failure — what my ruling should have specified
  instead of trusting a type annotation.
SERIALIZATION ANSWER, so nobody reintroduces (str, Enum) for a legitimate reason: (str, Enum) was presumably
  chosen so the value serializes as "destructive" for MCP/JSON. KEEP IntEnum FOR ORDERING; SERIALIZE
  EXPLICITLY via `effect.name.lower()`. NEVER RELY ON AN ENUM'S str-NESS FOR BOTH ORDERING AND WIRE FORMAT —
  two jobs, one type, which is the ONE-NAME-TWO-QUANTITIES shape hit four times this week, here inside a
  base class.
SEQUENCING: land IntEnum + the ordering assertion IN ONE COMMIT. A type change without the test is the same
  latent defect with a different literal.

