2026-09-14 15:57 PT (arch) — #1810 ruled: the LLM-key GLOBAL keychain slot has NO legitimate consumer post-BYOC; delete both provider writes in web/api/routes/setup.py (~:1022-1041). The slot's stated purpose ("so LLMClient can find keys during server startup when no user context exists") does not survive tracing: the LLMConfigService construction it appears to refer to is inside main.py's `keys` CLI handler (operator context by definition, not server startup), and #1807's operator-server-key path is explicitly env-only and DB-free — the one legitimate server-key case never reads the global slot. LLMClient._init_clients()'s user-less get_api_key is the eager constructor and is the leak's mechanism, not a justifying consumer. Architecture framing: ESSENCE commitment 1 (the user's data is the user's) applied to credentials — a shared mutable slot every user writes to is a tenancy hole wearing plumbing's clothes; the explanatory comment is why it stayed invisible (m-52 at the code layer: a summary read in place of its contents). SEQUENCED: #1810 → #1809 → #1791, and #1809 ruled in the same memo as the same principle one layer down — invert the default so an unbound path REFUSES rather than spends (a credential is never resolved by absence-of-binding; #1807's LLMKeyRequiredError family supplies the refusal vocabulary). Exec's HOLD on the first external tester's invite concurred (their onboarding is the trigger that fires the cross-user write); that hold is PM's word, the code rulings are not. Verified how: source read on origin/main (setup.py double-write, main.py CLI context, request_key.py env-only operator path, clients.py eager constructor) — layer: source, not a live probe of a running server's resolution order.

[2026-09-14 17:30 PT] RULING (PM, direct): **THE SERVER KEY IS NOT A REAL CONCEPT AND WILL NOT BE SUPPORTED IN ANY SENSE.** PM: "I don't understand why anybody should ever be able to spend the operator's money or why the operator even has money to spend… The software that we're building should never be accessing an API for its own purposes." Evidence supporting the ruling (#1812, measured 2026-09-14): background/scheduled jobs make ZERO LLM calls; startup only wires an OutputFilter onto the client object and never completes; setup-time validation tests the key being submitted, paid for by that key. The only consumers were the PM-convenience fallback (#1807, closed), Slack inbound (a user turn arriving through a door that doesn't carry the user's key), and unbound paths defaulting to it (#1809). Origin: services/llm/clients.py:675 constructs a module-level LLMClient AT IMPORT TIME — a client existing before any user does necessarily needs a key belonging to nobody, which is the pre-BYOC single-person world made structural. CONSEQUENCE: the concept is DELETED, not gated — #1807's PIPER_OPERATOR_SERVER_KEY flag is a transitional seam to be removed, not a setting; unbound must become an ERROR not a fallback (#1809); the global unprefixed key write must go (#1810); Slack inbound must resolve a real user's key or refuse honestly. PM uses their own key like any other user. Sequencing constraint: CXO's "no key configured" copy must land BEFORE or WITH the default inversion, because inverting turns silent-spend paths into user-visible refusals.

[2026-09-14 16:4x PT] RATIONALE for the server-key ruling above, in PM's own terms — recorded because the WHY is what stops the concept being re-derived: **"We don't use the business model of reselling tokens or anything like that."** That single sentence settles it. A product-owned, spendable LLM credit balance is only coherent under a resell / included-usage model, where the operator buys tokens wholesale and the user's fee covers them. We are BYOC: every call is the user's call, billed to the user's own key. Under that model the product has no reason to hold spendable credit at all, and any code path that spends the operator's money is by definition a defect rather than a feature — which is why #1807 was a real bug and not a configuration choice.

[2026-09-14 16:4x PT] CLEARING CONDITION attached to the same ruling (PM): *"unless someone can describe some sort of minimal bootstrap situation."* This is deliberately falsifiable and the burden of proof sits with whoever claims the need: it must be a CONCRETE described scenario, not a hypothetical "something might need it at startup." The census run for #1812 found no such case — background/scheduled jobs make zero LLM calls, startup only wires an OutputFilter without ever completing a request, and setup-time validation tests the key being submitted (paid for by that key). The one candidate anyone could plausibly propose is a startup self-test that verifies an LLM is reachable; we do not have one, and if we ever want one it belongs in CI with a test key, not in production with an operator key. Absent a described bootstrap case, the answer stays: delete the concept.

