2026-09-19 13:1x PT (arch) — PROVIDER-AGNOSTICISM AT THE TASK LAYER IS LOAD-BEARING, DERIVED FROM BYOC, AND UNENFORCED. Ruled in answer to CXO's explicitly-flagged question from the #1823 branch-two trace ("if provider-agnosticism is deliberate and load-bearing then branch two may be PERMANENTLY empty rather than merely empty today"). PPM's separate ruling that branch two is out of scope for #1823 stands unchanged; this is the layer underneath it. THREE PARTS. (1) DERIVED, NOT INDEPENDENT: provider-agnosticism is a consequence of BYOC, not a separate preference. If a task type could require a specific provider, a user whose only key is OpenAI could not run that task — #1823's complaint generalized from one endpoint to the whole task surface. PM's #1812 ruling ("we don't resell tokens; every call is the user's call, billed to the user's own key") makes BYOC the model, so provider-agnosticism is what keeps BYOC COHERENT. Trading it away breaks a ratified commitment rather than a nicety. (2) THEREFORE BRANCH TWO IS PERMANENTLY EMPTY — CONDITIONAL ON BYOC, and the conditional IS the answer, not a hedge appended to it: branch two cannot become non-empty without abandoning BYOC or knowingly breaking it for a subset of users, and neither should be reachable by a task-config PR. (3) IT IS NOT LAW — checked, not assumed: provider-agnosticism appears in NEITHER ESSENCE.md's five standing architectural rules NOR its seven commitments. It is protected today by exactly two things: one source comment (config.py:74) and the incidental fact that all three providers define all three tiers. A PR adding an Anthropic-tuned task type would break BYOC for every non-Anthropic user and nothing in the repo would fail, flag, or notice. DELIBERATELY NOT RECOMMENDING AN ESSENCE AMENDMENT: the law already exists as BYOC and a new commitment would restate an existing one at lower altitude — corpus growth that makes law harder to read without making it more binding, and it would spend a PM ratifi