e gate fires at web/api/routes/intent.py:562-577 BEFORE intent_service.process_intent(), deliberately per its own comments (#1807 "Refuse BEFORE intent_service/the LLM", #1320 "refuse BEFORE touching intent_service/the LLM at all") — but DISPOSITION IS DETERMINED BY THE PRE-CLASSIFIER INSIDE process_intent. So at gate time the system does not yet know the message is a greeting, and "let the greeting through" is NOT a condition that can be added to the existing gate; the gate structurally cannot see what it would condition on. CURE: run the pre-classifier BEFORE the gate and gate on the disposition. Verified because it is the hinge: PreClassifier is "Rule-based pre-classification", regex over the message string, NO LLM call anywhere in the file (the only llm mentions are comments about what falls through to the LLM classifier) — so moving it ahead of the gate SPENDS NOTHING, which is precisely the property the gate exists to protect. The gate's condition then becomes a property lookup, and the invariant is statable and checkable: THE GATE REFUSES ONLY WHAT WOULD SPEND. Ratchet required in the same PR (every CANONICAL pair reachable keyless, every non-CANONICAL not) — a property alone does not stop the first handler added without re-deriving the reason, which is the exact widening CXO predicted for the list form. (3) CONSEQUENCE FOR THE COPY, which unblocks CXO and CORRECTS their framing: a greeting is CANONICAL and therefore NEVER REACHES THE FLOOR (FLOOR is a different disposition), so the `due_reminders` directive shape in conversational_floor.py — which CXO correctly identified as a shipped production pattern for "proactively surface a state the user didn't ask about" — IS NOT ON THIS PATH and cannot carry the greeting's key-state line. The string is FIXED TEXT IN THE CANONICAL HANDLER. The pattern CXO found is real; it just isn't reachable from here. Cost is lower than the alternative: fixed text in a deterministic handler is simpler than a model-composed directi